# Ernie + Bert -- sandbox configuration.
#
# Copy this file to `ernie-test.env` (drop the `.example`) and fill in the
# values. Whoever set this up sends you the numbers; this file is the
# shape they go in, so nothing has to be reconstructed from a chat message.
#
# `ernie-test.env` is gitignored. This template is not -- keep every real
# value out of it.
#
#   copy ernie-test.env.example ernie-test.env      (Command Prompt)
#   cp   ernie-test.env.example ernie-test.env      (Git Bash)

# The bot's token. A password: it can post as the bot in that server.
DISCORD_TOKEN=

# The sandbox server. Never the production guild id.
DISCORD_GUILD_ID=

# The write guard. NOT a separate number: paste the SAME value you just put
# in DISCORD_GUILD_ID above, character for character. Two values that have to
# agree, rather than a boolean a stray setting could flip.
#
# Leave it blank or get it wrong and the preflight stops you with
#   Writes are off. ALLOW_DISCORD_WRITES must equal <the guild id>
# which is the whole point of it: otherwise your board looks fine and the
# other person never sees a thing.
ALLOW_DISCORD_WRITES=

# Whether this machine tells the customer thread what happened to it in
# Discord -- somebody archiving it, and somebody unarchiving it again.
# Both halves of the same fact, and both seen by every stack watching, so
# both are behind this one switch. Ernie says so in the thread the same way
# it does for a Complete pressed in Bert -- "JulianD closed this thread in
# Discord" -- which costs an unarchive, a message and a re-archive, so the
# thread comes back into everybody's sidebar for as long as that takes.
#
# Optional, and only ONE machine should set it -- this one, unlike
# CHANGELOG_CHANNEL_ID, which stopped being one-machine on 2026-09-18. The
# reason does not carry over: a change is made on one board and logged there,
# but every stack runs its own sync, every stack notices the same archived
# thread, and every stack writes its own closure row. Those rows cost nothing while they never post. Switch this on in two
# places and the thread is told twice. If you are the one joining, leave it
# empty.
#
# A pass that finds more than ANNOUNCE_MAX closed at once says nothing -- a
# stack started after a weekend is catching up, not watching, and a backlog
# announcing itself as news is worse than silence. Those closures still
# close their cards; what is held back is only the announcement.
#
# It gates reopens as well as closures. Both are "what happened to this thread
# in Discord", every stack sees both, and both are told to the thread the same
# way -- which is why this is one switch and not two. It was called
# ANNOUNCE_CLOSURES until 2026-09-17; that name is still read, with a warning.
#
# 1, true, yes or on. Anything else, including blank, is off.
ANNOUNCE_THREAD_CHANGES=

# Give a status message to threads Ernie inherited, not just ones it watched
# open. Off by default, and the default is the careful one: a first sync makes
# a card for every thread there has ever been.
#
# What makes it worth switching on is the number, not the rule. An archived
# thread is skipped anyway, separately, so the set this speaks into is the
# OPEN ones -- 40 of production's 927 on 2026-09-17, of which 37 were
# inherited and would otherwise never get one. Those are live tickets, and the
# board was carrying a status on 2 cards of 39.
#
# BACKFILL_MAX threads per pass, in board order, so it walks down the board
# over an hour instead of putting 37 notifications in everybody's sidebar at
# once. The log says how many are still to go.
#
# Safe on more than one machine, unlike CHANGELOG_CHANNEL_ID and
# ANNOUNCE_THREAD_CHANGES: a board with no row looks in the thread before it posts,
# so a second one adopts the first one's message rather than adding a second.
#
# 1, true, yes or on. Anything else, including blank, is off.
STATUS_BACKFILL=

# Wipe/seed target for seed_test_server.py and wipe_test.py.
TEST_CHANNEL_ID=

# Channels the sync mirrors, comma-separated. Cards channels generate cards
# on Bert's board; history channels are mirrored for search only and put
# nothing on the board.
#
# HISTORY_CHANNEL_IDS is usually empty -- the sandbox has no history channel.
# Leave it blank unless you were given one. Unlike CHANGELOG_CHANNEL_ID below
# that is not a rule, just nothing to fill in: the sync only ever reads, so
# two machines watching the same history channel duplicate nothing.
CARD_CHANNEL_IDS=
HISTORY_CHANNEL_IDS=

# Board state channel: one message per card, edited in place. This is what
# makes two machines one board. Without it you get a private board of your
# own and nothing is shared.
STATE_CHANNEL_ID=

# Durable record of every change. Optional, and **every** machine may set it.
# A change is made on exactly one board, so that board owns the line: nobody's
# uptime decides whether the record exists, and there is no machine to
# nominate. It was one machine until 2026-09-18, because both boards hold the
# whole history and two loggers wrote every line twice; events.replayed is
# what tells one board's change from its copy of the other's.
CHANGELOG_CHANNEL_ID=

# Log only the changes made on THIS machine, leaving the other board's to the
# other board.
#
# Off unless set, and off is the arrangement that loses nothing: a machine logs
# everything it holds, replays included, which is what the one nominated logger
# did before any of this. An upgrade that changes nothing records no less than
# it did.
#
# Switch it on only when **every** machine that logs has it on. Set on one and
# off on the other, the one that is on drops the other board's changes and the
# one that is off posts them twice. Two machines that both log and both have
# this on is the arrangement where nobody's uptime decides whether the record
# exists.
#
# 1, true, yes or on. Anything else, including blank, is off.
CHANGELOG_OWN_ONLY=

# The customer list, read from Jira so client names are picked in Bert rather
# than typed. Optional: leave all four blank and Bert's Client box stays the
# free-text box it always was.
#
# JIRA_TOKEN is an API token, not your password -- make one at
# id.atlassian.com > Security > API tokens. It is read-only here; nothing in
# this repository creates or edits a Jira issue.
#
# JIRA_CLIENT_JQL is the query that returns the Client CR issues. Check it
# with
#   python ernie_jira.py --check --env ernie-test.env --db ernie-test.db
# which fails if the query misses a client the board already uses -- worth
# doing, because the obvious narrow query returns only the first page.
# The instance and the account are not secrets, so they are filled in here.
JIRA_BASE_URL=https://edgeaisolutions.atlassian.net
JIRA_EMAIL=edge.ai.solutions@gmail.com

# Leave blank if ATLASSIAN_API_TOKEN is already set in your environment --
# jira_client.py reads that one, and this reads it as a fallback, so the
# token lives in exactly one place whichever way you set it.
JIRA_TOKEN=

# The query that returns the Client CR issues. Not a secret, and filled in
# because it is the one value here nobody could guess: `parent = PIP-2132` is
# the *Client Tracker* issue, whose 105 children are the customer list. The
# obvious query -- project = PIP AND issuetype = "Customer Requirement" --
# returns 129, of which 24 are product capabilities, trade shows and rejected
# junk that carry the type without being customers. Verified 105/105 against
# the list and 43/43 against the board.
JIRA_CLIENT_JQL=parent = PIP-2132 AND issuetype = "Customer Requirement" ORDER BY summary

# Where a newer Bert is downloaded from. Bert shows a "Get the new build"
# button on its update dialog only when this is set, so the control cannot
# exist without somewhere to go -- and because Ernie publishes it rather than
# Bert building it in, moving the code somewhere else later is this one line
# and a restart. Nobody holding a built Bert has to be sent a new one.
# http and https only; anything else is ignored.
BERT_UPDATE_URL=https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/16enP5SAdkA0MFZNQJEO34zwSii1BTMJH?usp=drive_link
